          "position": "I looked at WorkOrderly's description — 'SMB Workforce Scheduling.' That's uncomfortably close to 'field-service scheduling and dispatch for SMBs.' The overlap is at the product-category level, not just the customer level. That's the kind of conflict our LP agreements specifically prohibit."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I hear the concern, but let's be precise. WorkOrderly is a horizontal workforce scheduling tool — think shift workers, service staff rostering. Cadence appears to be vertical-specific: job-based dispatch for service trades (HVAC, plumbing, landscaping). Those are adjacent but meaningfully different buyer journeys. ServiceBridge also exited, which means we have direct expertise in the space without an active conflict there. I think we need a clean legal opinion on WorkOrderly and FieldForce before this becomes a deal-killer, but I don't want to dismiss Cadence on a conflict that may be definitionally narrower than it looks."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Fine — but FieldForce is 'Field Operations.' That is not a narrow overlap. We need the founders to explicitly differentiate from both WorkOrderly and FieldForce before we can pass this through compliance. That needs to happen before we go further on diligence."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "Agreed. The conflict resolution is a gate item. If WorkOrderly and FieldForce are real conflicts and the GPs can't waive them, this discussion is academic. Let's proceed with the business merits on the assumption we can resolve the conflict question, but it's the first thing that has to come back clean."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Market Size and Timing — Is This a Big Enough Bet?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Let me make the bull case on market first. There are 5-plus million service businesses in the US alone — HVAC, electrical, plumbing, landscaping, cleaning, pest control, pool service. The majority of them still dispatch through phone calls, text chains, and whiteboards. Software penetration in this segment is genuinely low. ServiceTitan has proven the high end of the market can be professionalized; Jobber has proven the mid-market exists. What's not well-served is the true micro-SMB — the 1-to-10 technician shop. That's the cohort Cadence seems to be targeting at $333 ARPU, and that's a large, underserved, and increasingly digitization-ready cohort."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "I don't dispute the TAM exists. My concern is the SAM. The micro-SMB field service segment has historically been graveyard territory for SaaS companies — high churn, price sensitivity, low LTV, high support burden. ServiceTitan's success at $50K-plus ACV is precisely because they avoided this segment. Jobber charges more than $333 per month for most of its value-added tiers. Is Cadence pricing into a segment that's too small per customer to support a venture-scale business?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "That's the right question, but I think you're anchoring on the wrong comp. The correct analog is Toast in restaurants or Mindbody in fitness — vertical SaaS products that entered at low ARPU, built density, then expanded upmarket and into payments and financial services. Cadence's $333/month scheduling wedge could be the first touch in a much larger platform play. If they expand into job management, invoicing, customer communications, and eventually payments — which is how ServiceTitan became a multi-billion dollar company — the LTV per customer expands dramatically."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I want that vision conversation to be real, not just a pitch. Toast took eight years and hundreds of millions of dollars to execute that platform expansion. The question is whether Cadence's founders have actually thought through that journey, or whether they're just describing their scheduling tool with a $1B narrative attached. The vision needs to come from the founders, not from us projecting it onto them."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Team Composition — Can Two Technical Founders Win This Market?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "This is my hardest concern. Ex-Stripe engineer and ex-Notion PM are genuinely impressive credentials. But I've seen this pattern fail specifically in SMB trades markets. The buyers they need to reach — HVAC operators, plumbing contractors — they don't read Product Hunt, they don't respond to cold email sequences, they trust referrals from their trade association and their supply house. Neither of these founders has that network. Who is going to be their first commercial hire, and how are they going to penetrate the distribution channels that matter in this vertical?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I partially disagree with the premise. The ex-Notion PM background is actually a strong signal for this specific market. Notion succeeded in an unsexy productivity market against well-funded incumbents by making the product so delightful that users became evangelists. Field service software is notorious for being clunky — ServiceTitan's UI is famously difficult. If Cadence's product is genuinely beautiful and frictionless, the word-of-mouth in tight-knit trade communities could be enormous. Plumbers talk to other plumbers. An HVAC operator who loves Cadence will tell every other HVAC operator at their regional association meeting."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "That's a valid path to the first 200 customers. It's not a path to 5,000. At some point they need a commercial leader who understands channel sales into this market — trade distributors, franchise systems, insurance carriers who want their contractors to be efficient. Neither founder brings that. The seed round needs to include a clear plan for building commercial capacity, and the $4M raise has to be explicitly sized to include that hire within the first six months."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Exactly. The test I want to run: ask them who their first commercial hire is. If they can name a specific person or describe a specific profile with a clear rationale, that tells me they have thought seriously about the go-to-market gap. If they say 'we'll find someone after we close the round,' that tells me they haven't."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Unit Economics and Valuation — Does the Math Work?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "Let me walk through the numbers as presented. $40K MRR, 120 customers — that's $333 ARPU. $1,200 CAC, 80% gross margins. Simple payback is $1,200 divided by ($333 times 0.80), which is about 4.5 months. That's excellent — genuinely best-in-class for SMB SaaS. The problem is I don't believe the $1,200 CAC is fully loaded. Two technical founders closing deals personally is not free. If we allocate even 20% of each founder's time to sales at a market-rate cost basis, and add onboarding and support costs, the real CAC is probably $2,500-$4,000. That changes the payback to 9-15 months — still fine, but not spectacular."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Agreed on the CAC caveat. The other number I'm staring at is the 33x ARR multiple. $16M pre on $480K ARR. For a two-person technical team with four months of growth data and no disclosed retention proof, that's a premium price. In the current market, strong seed-stage vertical SaaS is pricing at 15-20x ARR. What is the premium being paid for here — the team pedigree? The growth rate?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "The premium is being paid for the growth rate and the gross margin profile together. An 18% MoM growth rate compounding forward is rare. If they sustain even 12% MoM over the next 12 months, they are at $1.4M ARR by Series A. At a 20x multiple, that is a $28M pre. We are getting in at $16M now. The math works if the growth is durable — and durability is exactly what the diligence needs to establish."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "That's the right frame. The valuation is defensible if the cohort data shows the growth is sustainable rather than front-loaded. But I need to see that data before I can endorse the entry price. If churn is running 3% monthly, the customer base has a 33-month implied half-life, and the LTV math gets ugly fast. If churn is under 1% monthly and there is any expansion revenue, the numbers look very different. We simply do not know which world we are in."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Competitive Moat — What Keeps Cadence Defensible?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "The thing I keep coming back to is the question of what the moat is. ServiceTitan has workflow lock-in, Jobber has brand awareness and integrations. What does Cadence have at 120 customers? The honest answer right now is: a better product and some early customers. That is a starting position, not a moat. The moat question is what happens at 1,000 customers or 5,000. Does the product get better as more customers use it? Are there network effects in a marketplace for field service workers? Do they collect data that becomes a competitive asset?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "The one credible moat path I see for a tool like this is integration depth. If Cadence becomes the system of record for a contractor — meaning it connects to their accounting software, their supplier ordering, their customer communication, their payment processing — switching cost becomes enormous. But that requires a platform roadmap and it requires commercial relationships with adjacent vendors. Is the team building toward that or building a best-in-class scheduling feature that Jobber can replicate in a quarter?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "There is also a data moat opportunity that I do not think they have articulated. If Cadence is processing scheduling and dispatch data across thousands of service businesses, they accumulate proprietary data on technician productivity, job completion rates, seasonal demand patterns, customer satisfaction correlations. That data could power pricing recommendations, staffing optimization, or insurance products — none of which exist in the market today. But someone needs to ask whether the founders are thinking about data as a strategic asset."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "That data angle is actually what makes me most excited about this company. If you aggregate dispatch, job completion, technician performance, and customer outcome data across 10,000 SMB service businesses, you have something no one else has. But right now that is a hypothesis, not a strategy. I want to hear the founders talk about data as a moat before I get excited about it on their behalf."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Consensus and Decision",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Alright — where are we landing? My position is conditional more diligence. The execution signals are strong enough that I do not want to pass outright. But I need to see channel attribution data, cohort retention, and a credible commercial hire plan before I can recommend investing. The conflict check on WorkOrderly and FieldForce also needs to clear compliance."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "Same. The numbers are promising but incomplete. I need fully-loaded unit economics, cohort retention curves, burn rate, and a valuation comp set before I can underwrite the 33x ARR multiple. I am not a pass — the gross margins and growth rate are real signals. But I am also not a clean invest without those answers."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Aligned. I want to do this deal — I think the timing is right and the team has genuine product DNA for a market that has never had great software. But I need the founders to articulate the 10-year platform vision clearly, demonstrate that the 18% growth is structural rather than a warm-network burst, and resolve the conflict question. If all three of those come back well, I would sponsor this investment with conviction. More diligence is the right call for now."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Let's give it three weeks. Specific asks: portfolio conflict resolution via compliance, channel-by-channel CAC breakdown, cohort retention data for all cohorts, burn rate and financial model, and a 60-minute founder session on the 10-year vision and first commercial hire. If they can turn all of that in two weeks, we reconvene and make a final call."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "consensus_verdict": "more_diligence",
  "key_concerns": [
    "Portfolio conflict with WorkOrderly and FieldForce must be resolved through legal/compliance before investment can proceed",
    "GTM motion is opaque — no channel-level acquisition data, no evidence of repeatable non-founder-led acquisition",
    "No commercial co-founder creates a structural GTM gap for a domain that requires relationship-driven, vertical-channel sales",
    "Fully-loaded unit economics are unverified — stated $1,200 CAC excludes founder time and onboarding labor",
    "No cohort retention data — churn rate, GRR, and NDR are all undisclosed, making the 33x ARR valuation hard to underwrite",
